@meta
id: 02-04
title: Writing to a different filename
chapter: 02
type: lesson
skills: write, quit
par: 36
minutes: 4
vscode: File > Save As
open: runbook.md

@brief
`:w` takes an argument. Give it a filename and the buffer is written
there instead of to the file you opened. The file you opened is not
touched, and the buffer keeps its original name — you have taken a copy,
not moved house.

The payments runbook needs a version for the 2.14 deploy. The title line
should read:

    # Payments runbook 2.14

Make that change and write it to `runbook-2.14.md`. `runbook.md` itself is
the live document for the team and must come out of this byte for byte
unchanged. Change it and the check fails.

@keys
| `:w name` | write the buffer to `name`, leaving the current file alone |
| `:saveas name` | write to `name` and make it the buffer's new name |
| `:q!` | quit without writing |

@hints
Edit the title in the buffer first. Nothing has touched `runbook.md` yet
— an unwritten buffer is just memory.
--
`:w` on its own writes to `runbook.md`, which is the one thing you must
not do. Give `:w` the new name instead.
--
After `:w runbook-2.14.md` the buffer still belongs to `runbook.md` and
still counts as modified, so `:q` refuses. You do not want that write.
`:q!`.

@solution
    $ a  2.14 <Esc> :w runbook-2.14.md<Enter> :q!<Enter>

Two things are worth pulling out of that.

First, `:w name` is how you fork a file mid-edit. Reworking a config and
not sure the rework is better? `:w config.new.ts`, keep editing, compare
them outside vim later.

Second, the `:q!` at the end is not a failure. The buffer was still
`runbook.md` and still modified, so vim was right to refuse a plain `:q`.
If you want the buffer to *become* the new file, `:saveas runbook-2.14.md`
renames it as it writes, and then `ZZ` does what you would expect.

@start runbook.md
# Payments runbook

Owner: payments team
Pager: #payments-oncall

## Rollback

1. Flip the feature flag off
2. Redeploy the previous image
3. Tell the oncall channel

@expected runbook-2.14.md
# Payments runbook 2.14

Owner: payments team
Pager: #payments-oncall

## Rollback

1. Flip the feature flag off
2. Redeploy the previous image
3. Tell the oncall channel
